## Special Rules & Edge Cases

### Powers, costs and information

Access a card's power if you rule it or your pawn is at its site; relic holders use held relics. Persistent powers (thick black braid) affect the players specified regardless of ordinary access. Sites specify their own use conditions. Chancellor must apply uncovered Reliquary modifiers; title holder must apply title power.

Pay the braid/text cost. Normally cannot place favor/secrets on a card already holding either; battle-plan payment is the exception, not a license to Muster/Trade there. If power has no cost you can still use it despite existing resources. When paying outside your turn, favor goes immediately to matching bank; secrets stay on your board **facedown**. Facedown secrets cannot pay any place or burn cost until flipped up. Burn sends prompted resource to shared bank. Kill sends warband to its color's personal bank (purple to Chancellor). Sacrifice means choose to kill own warbands.

Resolve as much of used power as possible except optional “may” parts; may pay to use a power even if its effect does nothing. Cannot give away/burn/sacrifice extra resources beyond prompted cost or amount.

Faceup **tree** cards are site-only; **person** cards adviser-only; restrictions also govern moves/swaps. **Locked** cards cannot be discarded, moved or swapped once faceup except in Chronicle. Cards reducing adviser limit require discard based on **new limit before playing**; with multiple limit-changers use lowest, not sum. Respect locks.

Ordinary major-action modifiers are chosen at action start, used once per action, in any order; cannot discard an active modifier during that action. No-cost modifier may be used on multiple distinct actions. Must/cannot modifiers are mandatory. Battle plans instead occur in Campaign step 3, attacker first; each once per Campaign. Wake/Rest powers occur once at their stated phase; When Played is mandatory after site favor, Homeland effects and modifiers.

Rules priority: read literally; Law supersedes Playbook/aids except Site Reference; non-italic rules override italic reminders. “Must” overrides ordinary rules; “cannot” overrides even “must”; “ignore” overrides named must/cannot restrictions. “Must if able” has no penalty when unable. Promises are normally nonbinding, and pieces cannot simply be transferred without permission in a rule; Citizenship offers and expressly binding powers are exceptions. Tribunal disputed breach is settled by coin flip. Give and Take are distinct keywords; restricting one does not automatically restrict the other.

Component limits: favor/warbands/cards limited to available pieces; take/gain as much as possible if source short. Secrets and dice unlimited; use multipliers or roll again if needed. Banks/boards unlimited capacity unless explicitly limited. Facedown advisers can be inspected by holder and shown to others; their identity may be discussed or lied about. Private: facedown card fronts, discard fronts, world-deck count, world-deck backs below top. Public: discard counts/backs and board-resource/warband counts, and all other information. Keep facedown advisers distinct. Playbook group agreements about undoing actions or sharing drawn hands are social options, not additional mandatory rules.

### Site powers (Site Reference)

- **Opportunity** (Salt Flats/Mine/Drowned City): at end of your Wake take 1 favor/secret from site, optionally.
- **Homeland** sites: when you play a matching-suit card there and have not discarded a card there this turn, gain printed favor from matching bank, 2 warbands, or take its relic as Recover, as shown. A relic Recover still pays normal relic cost but no Supply.
- **Coasts:** traveling from one Coast to another costs 1 Supply and ignores Narrow Pass.
- **Plains/Mountain:** must add/subtract 1 attack die when site or anything at site is targeted, even if attacker rules it.
- **River:** Muster there gains 1 additional warband if you rule it.
- **Charming Valley:** traveling from it increases Travel cost by 1.
- **Shrouded Wood:** travel from it costs 2, ignoring Narrow Pass and Hidden Place; if enemy rules it, travel to site of ruler's choice (Chancellor chooses for Empire), even when someone else makes you travel.
- **Narrow Pass:** entering its region from another must travel to it; targeting anything in its region from another region must also target it unless you rule it.
- **Great Slum:** when playing/moving a card there, may first discard a card there.
- **Marshes:** Search while pawn there draws 1 fewer card.
- **Buried Giant:** traveling from it may flip 1 own board secret facedown to spend no Supply and ignore Narrow Pass.
- **Hidden Place:** cannot travel there or declare Campaign targets there unless flipping 1 board secret facedown.
- **Tribunal:** Action if you rule it or pawn there: negotiate binding exchanges of favor, secrets, relics and banners, including combined future terms and penalties.

**Bandit Crown:** treat bandits as own warbands: rule empty faceup sites, may pick up last physical site warband, add relevant bandits to defense (or attacking force with suitable powers). Bandits themselves cannot be killed, moved or sacrificed, including skulls or battle-plan costs.

### Writing the Chronicle (Law §8, with base errata)

Winner performs these steps in order. After first game open Archive packet: place 8 dividers; put edifices in matching sections; separately shuffle each suit's denizens into its Archive section.

1. **Vow an Oath:** won by Vision alone → next Oath matches that Vision. Any other way or multiple victory methods → choose any Oath **except current one**. Save goal in World Box.
2. **Citizenship:** if winner Exile, flip **all** Citizen boards, including boxed, to Exile. May offer Citizenship to other Exiles except those just flipped; ignore normal in-play offer cost/relic rules. Acceptors flip Citizen and replace all garrisons at sites they rule with **one winner-color warband per site**. Winner cannot offer themself Chronicle Citizenship.
3. **Map and edifices:** Imperial winner may build **or** repair **one** edifice. At ruled site with no intact or ruined edifice, replace denizen with matching intact Archive edifice, returning denizen to world deck; or flip a ruled ruin intact. Discard all sites except those winner rules or with an intact edifice; shuffle discarded sites into site deck, discard their denizens, return relics to relic deck and ruined edifices to Archive. At retained sites winner does not rule, flip intact edifice to ruin and discard all denizens there. Set aside **all sites with ruins**, with attached ruins, remaining denizens and relics, in map order top Cradle to bottom Hinterland. Remove pawns/favor/secrets/warbands from map.
4. **Repack map slots:** excluding set-aside ruins, fill Cradle top-down by pushing Cradle sites up then importing Provinces then Hinterland in top-down order. Fill Provinces top-down by pushing up then importing Hinterland top-down. Push Hinterland sites up. Fill empty Hinterland slots **bottom-up** with set-aside ruins **bottom-up**, then empty Provinces slots similarly. Fill any remaining slots with facedown site-deck cards; in each region with no faceup site, flip top one up.
5. **Add 6 denizens:** winner's most common adviser suit determines 3 random Archive cards, next clockwise suit 2, next clockwise suit 1, to world deck. Choose among tied suits; no suited advisers → choose any. Suit cycle Discord→Arcane→Order→Hearth→Beast→Nomad→Discord. If Archive cannot supply the full allocation, do **not** add that allocation: heal Archive instead. Sort all Dispossessed by suit; take 6 random from largest Dispossessed suit stack into world deck; shuffle remaining Dispossessed into matching Archive suit stacks.
6. **Dispossess 6:** set aside all 5 Visions including Conspiracy. Shuffle all discard piles with **losers' advisers**, move 6 random cards unseen to Dispossessed. Winner's advisers are excluded from this pool.
7. **Relics:** save Grand Scepter in World Box; return losing players' relics to relic deck and shuffle. Refill faceup sites with fewer relics than printed R icons up to printed number, facedown. Shuffle winner's remaining relics with Reliquary relics and stack atop relic deck.
8. **Save world:** stack each site's attached cards on it, stack sites so top Cradle on top and bottom Hinterland at bottom, put in World Box. Save player boards on current Exile/Citizen sides.
9. **Rebuild world deck:** shuffle all denizens remaining in play (not those retained at sites/Archive/Dispossessed). Top stack **10 denizens + 2 random Visions = 12**; middle **15 denizens + remaining 3 Visions = 18**; remaining denizens bottom. Save in Archive World Deck section. Chronicle aid's printed “12 denizens + 2” is incorrect; do not make a 14-card top stack.

## Player Reference

| Action | Supply | Main additional cost/result |
|---|---:|---|
| Search | 2–4 | Up to 3 cards; world Vision stops draw; discard then play at most 1 |
| Muster | 1 | 1 favor on empty site card → 2 board warbands |
| Trade | 1 | 1 secret → 1+matching advisers favor; 2 favor → matching advisers secrets |
| Recover | 1 | Site relic cost or strictly outbid banner resources |
| Campaign | 2 | Declare local target, may add remote sites; attack must exceed defense |
| Travel | 1–4 | Move pawn; reveal and stock facedown site |
| Minor / Action power | 0 | Apply printed resource costs and permissions |

Wake: feed People's Favor → Exile victory → flip title → opportunity site. Rest: return favor → return/ready secrets → refresh → save unspent Supply → Rest powers. End round 5/6/7: Empire Oathkeeper permits end roll 6/5–6/3–6. Round 8: Empire Oathkeeper → Usurper → Vision (Conquest/Rebellion/Sanctuary/Faith) → Empire fallback; Citizen Successor replaces any Chancellor win.

Sources: [Law of Oath, October 20, 2020](https://buriedgiant.com/oath/Oath_Law_of_Oath.pdf), corresponding Playbook pp. 2–32, [Site Reference](https://buriedgiant.com/oath/Oath_Reference_Sheet.pdf), and [official base-game clarifications and errata, November 15, 2022](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1p2tHzbTmQhU8tPfZeK5gSJvmfScVFUeepFgIRfCpyuw/edit). Card-specific powers remain on their cards.


FILE errata.txt
﻿Oath Clarifications and Errata
Last updated: November 15, 2022


If you have any questions that are not answered here or in the forums, you can submit a question to us here.


Law Clarifications


1.17. Relic Setup.
Do the four relics that go in the Imperial Reliquary get placed at random, or do I get to choose which relic goes on which space?
They are placed randomly on the Imperial Reliquary spaces.


2.11. Oathkeeper Title. 
Is the defense die added from the Oathkeeper title considered a campaign modifier?
Yes! 


Since players both lose rule (5.5.6) and gain rule (5.5.7) of sites during a Campaign action, when exactly do I transfer the Oathkeeper of Supremacy title?
At the end of the Campaign action. (I may add an errata below, but I’m figuring out whether there’s a better wording.)


3.2. Visionary Win. 
When a Vision says I need the “most sites” or “most relics and banners,” does that include ties?
Nope! In order to qualify for, you need to have more than each other player, not tied.


4.3.3. Refresh Supply.
As a Citizen, do I refresh to the exact space of the Chancellor’s Supply marker, or do I count up the Chancellor’s warbands? Also, what happens with the leftmost space? There’s no Chancellor mask on my leftmost Supply space.
You refresh to the exact space of the Chancellor’s Supply marker, so if their marker is in the leftmost space (good on them), you refresh to the leftmost space. For clarity, that space should have the Chancellor mask.


5.4. Recover.
Can I recover the Banner of the Darkest Secret if the holder’s site has no cards?
Nope! Likewise, you cannot do so if there is only a ruin at the site, since ruins do not have suits.


Can I recover the Banner of the Darkest Secret if there is a suited card at the holder’s site, but the holder has no advisers?
Yes!


5.5.2. Step 2: Declare Targets and Collect Dice Pools (Campaign).
If the defender’s pawn is at my site, does targeting one of their relics fulfill the “you must declare at least one target at your site” requirement?
Yes it does. This use of “at your site” is a little inconsistent with other uses that refer to things that are physically on the map at the site, so I will likely tweak this wording to clarify in future printings.


5.6. Travel.
How exactly do Travel costs get modified?
First, check for any modifications of the Travel cost to a specific number, such as the Shrouded Wood. Then, modify it with any other Travel modifiers, such as the Decadent power on the Imperial Reliquary. Any Travel modifier that says “Spend no Supply” completely overrides the Travel cost (7.6.2).


7.1.3. Use.
Does “as much as possible” imply that I can use a power even if it would have no effect?
Yes! For example, you could put a favor on Sleight of Hand to pay for it even if there was no one around to take a secret from.


7.5.3. Pay Limit. 
The clarification says I can only use a battle plan “once.” What does that mean?
It means you cannot use the same battle plan multiple times in a single Campaign action.


Glossary. “Give” and “Take” entries.
If some power says, for example, that I cannot “give secrets,” does that mean that other players cannot take secrets from me?
Nope! The “give” and “take” keywords are distinct, and something modifying how you give does not modify how you or others take.


Rules Errata and Additions


Law
2.8. Site Cards.
Add the following text “Facedown sites cannot hold cards.”
(Even though the original rules largely prevented cards from being placed at facedown sites, there were a couple sneaky ways to get around this, so it’s best to make this explicit.)


5.4.1. Recover, Spend 1 Supply and Choose Target. Change the following clarification in error: “(Ignore ruined edifices and facedown advisers, since they have no suit. You cannot recover the Darkest Secret from another player if their site has no cards, or if they have no faceup advisers, but you can recover it if they have no faceup advisers.)”


5.5 Campaign. There is some confusion about how to interpret Imperial Allies’ power sharing permissions, and timing. Use this guidance to interpret them:


Interpret uses of “you,” “your enemy,” “the defender,” etc. on powers to include the entire side—the declared defender and any Allies. However, "your site" and "your board" still refer specifically to the site of your pawn and your own board. Here are some examples:
* Mercenaries: “you’re defeated” -> “your side is defeated” 
* True Names: “against you” -> “against your side” 
* Gleaming Armor: “Your enemy’s” -> “The enemy side’s” 
* Specialist: “the defender” -> “the defending side” 
* Jinx: “you roll” -> “your side rolls”
* Oathkeeper Bonus: "the defender" -> "the defending side" (Meaning this bonus is shared among the Chancellor and Imperial Allies on defense in non-Supremacy games.)
* Peace Envoys, Code of Honor: "you cannot use other battle plans" -> "your side cannot use other battle plans"
* Warning Signals: No change. Read as written.


In rare situations where the defender and/or Allies disagree about timing, priority, or order of who can use a battle plan and when, the declared defender chooses the order. 
(For example, if two players want to use the same power, the declared defender chooses who gets to use the power. If a player might or might not choose to use Code of Honor, which would restrict other battle plans from being used, the declared defender can force them to say whether they are using Code of Honor or not before the declared defender chooses which battle plans they are using.)


5.5.2 Step 2: Declare Targets and Collect Dice Pools. Edit the last sentence to end with “in their FORCE (on their board).” (This just helps with interpretation of powers like Captains.)


5.5.7.I Take Rule of Any Targeted Sites. The Empire box in this section should be moved to and combined with the Empire box in 5.5.6. (This should not affect resolution. It’s just a bit out of place.) 


7.6.4 Adviser Limits. Some powers (Assassin, Family Wagon, etc.) change your adviser limit. When you want to play a card that changes your adviser limit, check whether to discard advisers (5.1.4.II) based on your new adviser limit. If you have multiple cards that change your adviser limit, your adviser limit is the lowest among them. (They don’t add up.)


8.2 Offer Citizenship. Add “other” to “any other Exiles” 


8.3.3 Chronicle. Edit “their attached ruin and relic cards” to “their attached ruin, denizen, and relic cards” (This only matters in the rare situation where a ruin ends up ruled in a later game.)


10 Glossary. 
Draw: Edit to “Take cards from the top of the prompted draw source.” (The previous text was causing overspecification issues in other places, and the only card where the previous text mattered is Oracle. When you use Oracle, you should still advance the Visions Drawn track.)


You: Remove this entry. You: You alone, not including any Imperial Allies (5.5.2).
(This is causing some issues with power sharing. My apologies!)


Enemy: Add this text “Bandits are enemies to all players and vice versa.”


Rule: Add “face-up” to “players rule face-up sites” and “Bandits rule face-up sites that have no warbands.”


Chronicle Aid
The Rebuild Deck step says to shuffle 2 Visions and 12 denizens together to form the top stack. This is incorrect—the top stack should contain 2 Visions and 10 denizens for a total stack size of 12. The Law setup, Chronicle rules, and reference on the World Deck space of the map are all correct.


Clockwork Prince Rules
The example in Search and Play One Card should say “Discord card,” not “Discard card.”


Princely Reliquary
There are some minor differences in wording between some of the powers listed on this side of the Reliquary and the Imperial Reliquary side. They are equivalent, but consider the wording on the Imperial Reliquary side to be canon.


The Grand Scepter
The aid text on the back of the Grand Scepter should note that you can also exchange banners in a Citizenship offer, as described in the Law 6.6.1.




Changelog
11/15/2022: Added edits to 5.5.2 Step 2: Declare Targets and Collect Dice Pools and 7.6.4 Adviser Limits.
"}

